    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a high-ceiling core, anchored by **Yordan Alvarez** (85.83) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (77.16). **Yordan Alvarez** is our premier play, benefiting from a 4.68 xERA matchup against Bryce Miller and a robust +17% boost derived from the starter\u2019s skill profile. While **Pete Crow-Armstrong** carries a lower base projection, his efficiency is amplified by a high +14% platoon edge and the favorable matchup against JR Ritchie, making him a critical engine for our scarcity-driven SB strategy. **Trea Turner** (68.40) also remains an essential start, bolstered by a +10% batting order advantage and beneficial wind conditions, which help mitigate the threat of Sonny Gray on the mound.\n\nStrategic positioning continues to favor aggressive base-stealing profiles, evidenced by the inclusion of **Luke Raley** (62.37). **Luke Raley** is a high-reward start today because the opposing pitcher, Lance McCullers Jr., features a slow delivery that projects to provide a +4% boost to our stolen base probability. Conversely, our toughest personnel decision involved keeping **Casey Schmitt** (44.37) on the bench. Despite a solid +11% platoon edge, **Casey Schmitt** was ultimately squeezed out by the sheer efficiency of **Junior Caminero** (59.17) and **Caleb Durbin** (57.80); the optimizer prioritized the higher projected counting stats from the active middle infield group over the situational utility **Casey Schmitt** offered against a tough arm in Shohei Ohtani.\n\nOwners should maintain caution regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" statuses for **Gary Sanchez** and **Kazuma Okamoto**. **Gary Sanchez** is currently sitting on a marginal score (48.15) heavily influenced by a -3% platoon penalty, while **Kazuma Okamoto** faces a significant -19% handicap due to the elite skill level of Griffin Jax. Should the final lineups reveal these players are bumped down the order or facing unexpected shifts, we may need to pivot. We are